{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Cross-epic file-impact coordination (from RP-02 execution, 2026-08-18).\n\nRP-02 (epic 5e64-d9cd-2dd4-4036, citric-moudly-vipersquid) is decomposing binding_store.py into repository/lifecycle/recovery owners behind an unchanged facade, and is executing now. Three files in this task's impact set are being restructured underneath it:\n\n1. binding_store.py:110 `_env_int()` — reads os.environ for the binding lifecycle GC knobs (_DEFAULT_ABSENT_RETIRE_GRACE = 3 and the grace window). RP-02 S2 (fc1c-1b08-70c2-4afb, brainy-cooked-hog) MOVES this into the new src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/binding_lifecycle.py. After S2 lands, this task's recorded target file for that read is stale — retarget to binding_lifecycle.py, symbol _env_int. RP-02 deliberately preserves the read's sourcing behavior byte-identically (still ambient, same defensive parse, same default/clamp) rather than pre-empting the seam cut, which is this task's slice. Note that the value feeds the three-miss + 3600-second keyless-unbind conjunction, whose behavior RP-02 S3 pins by acceptance criterion and drives through the environment in tests; a cut to an operation-snapshot source needs those tests updated in the same change.\n\n2. apply_handlers.py:210-214 `_rich_cutover_active()` — reads load_config().reconciler.rich_text_cutover. RP-02 S3 T3 rewrites the IMMEDIATELY ADJACENT _observe_rich_reemit (:223-273) to stop mutating through all_bindings(). Semantically independent, but adjacent hunks; RP-02 is keeping its diff tight to _observe_rich_reemit and not reflowing _rich_cutover_active.\n\n3. run_differs.py:685-688 max_acting_fraction — RP-02 S3 T2 inserts a pre-observation recovery call early in the pass (after preflight/lock, before remote fetch), so the edits are textually disjoint. Shared risk is the module cap: the file is 739/800, so both slices are drawing on 61 lines of headroom.\n\nRecorded as relates_to, NOT depends_on: per docs/ticket-model.md:184-190 a blocking relation between a task here and a story under RP-02 would escalate to a full RP-04-epic <-> RP-02-epic blocker. Same reasoning RP-02 S3 already documented for its RP-04 S3 coupling. Overlapping file impact stays visible without freezing either root.\n\nNo pause requested. RP-02 lands first by design — this task cannot correctly cut a read whose home is about to move, and RP-02 relieves the 799/800 pressure on binding_store.py that currently blocks anyone from adding a line to it."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787096348230815001,"uuid":"21df44c1-9ca7-4a0d-8d04-fc14411fd1f9"}